# LandingOS — environment variables template.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values before starting the dev
# server:
#
#   cp .env.example .env        (Linux / macOS)
#   copy .env.example .env      (Windows CMD)
#
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DATABASE_URL  (REQUIRED)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PostgreSQL connection string. The app used SQLite originally, but a database
# file only survives where the filesystem does — and the Render free tier wipes
# the container on every deploy AND every idle spin-down, which silently
# destroyed orders and settings within hours. The Prisma provider is now
# "postgresql" and the container refuses to start without this variable.
#
# Neon (https://neon.tech) is the expected host: free tier, no card required,
# and it stays up. To get the string: create a project → Connection Details →
# copy the connection string. It looks like:
#
#   postgresql://user:password@ep-xxx-yyy.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require
#
# Use the DIRECT (non-pooled) string. This app runs as one long-lived container
# and applies its schema with `prisma db push` on startup, both of which prefer
# a direct connection over PgBouncer. Neon's pooled "-pooler" host is only
# worth switching to if you later hit connection limits.
#
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT: point this at a SEPARATE database, not production — a
# Neon branch is the easiest way (Neon → Branches → New Branch), since it gives
# you an isolated copy with its own connection string. Running `prisma db push`
# or the demo seed against production would otherwise touch real orders.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DATABASE_URL=

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AUTH_SECRET
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HMAC secret for signing admin session JWTs. REQUIRED for authentication.
# Without it the login endpoint returns HTTP 500 AUTH_SECRET_MISSING.
#
# Generate a secure random value with one of:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# Never commit the real .env file.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
AUTH_SECRET=

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADMIN_USERNAME / ADMIN_PASSWORD  (optional, used by the database seed)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Credentials for the seeded admin account. Used only on first boot when the
# database is created. Defaults to admin / admin123 — fine for local preview,
# but for a PUBLIC deployment set a strong, unique password here.
#
# If ADMIN_PASSWORD is set to anything other than "admin123", the account is
# created with mustChangePassword=false so you can log straight in.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin123

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CLOUDFLARE R2  (image storage — strongly recommended in production)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where uploaded product images are stored.
#
# Leave these BLANK for local development: uploads fall back to the local
# directory (data/uploads) and everything works with no cloud account.
#
# SET THEM in production on any host with an ephemeral filesystem — including
# Render's free tier, where the container is wiped on every deploy AND every
# time the instance spins down after inactivity. Without R2 there, uploaded
# images disappear within hours.
#
# Where to find each value in the Cloudflare dashboard (R2 → Overview):
#   R2_ACCOUNT_ID        Account ID shown on the R2 overview page
#   R2_BUCKET            The bucket name you created
#   R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID     R2 → Manage API Tokens → Create token (Object
#   R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY Read & Write). Both values are shown ONCE — copy them.
#
# R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL is OPTIONAL:
#   - Left blank, the bucket stays private and images are proxied through the
#     app. Simplest setup, works immediately.
#   - Set to a public bucket URL (Settings → Public Development URL, looks
#     like https://pub-xxxx.r2.dev) or a custom domain, image requests are
#     redirected straight to Cloudflare's CDN — faster, and image traffic no
#     longer counts against the app host's bandwidth.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET=
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# PRODUCTION DEPLOY (Render / Railway / Fly.io — Docker-based hosts)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The Dockerfile runs the app as a Next.js standalone server. It keeps NO
# durable state inside the container — the database is external Postgres and
# images go to R2 — so it runs on a host with no persistent disk at all.
#
# Set these on your host (Render → your service → Environment):
#
#   DATABASE_URL      — REQUIRED. External Postgres (see the top of this file).
#                       The container refuses to boot without it.
#   AUTH_SECRET       — REQUIRED. Random 32+ byte string (see command above).
#                       Also the key that decrypts stored Meta CAPI tokens, so
#                       CHANGING IT makes saved pixel tokens undecryptable.
#   ADMIN_USERNAME    — your chosen admin username (e.g. "admin")
#   ADMIN_PASSWORD    — a strong password for the admin account
#   R2_*              — all four required together (see the R2 section above),
#                       otherwise uploads silently fall back to local disk and
#                       images are lost on every restart.
#   SKIP_DEMO_SEED    — set to 1 once the store has real products, so the demo
#                       seeder stops refilling content you deleted.
#   PORT              — the host sets this; do not set it yourself.
#
# Verify a deploy landed correctly:
#
#   curl -s https://YOUR-APP.onrender.com/api/health
#
# It reports {"storage":{"backend":"r2"|"local", ...}}. "local" in production
# means the R2 variables did not take effect.
#
# Self-hosting on a box with a real disk instead? You can still mount a volume
# at /app/data to hold uploads (the local fallback path), but the database is
# now always external.

